Chapter 2 Rewriting With Variables
This chapter begins with a focus on the use of variables, such as x and y. First, you will use tools called algebra tiles to explore how and where to use variables. You will write expressions and then learn how to write them in the most efficient way.
Later in the chapter, you will learn how to compare two expressions to determine which one is greater. This will lead to a process of recording your steps so that anyone can understand your work. Finally, you will look at what happens when you cannot tell which expression is greater and consider the question, “What if they are equal?”
Since the topics in this chapter lay the foundation for rewriting expressions and solving equations, they will be revisited and built upon throughout the course. You will continue to make Connections Across Representations in your work with Linear Relationships.
In this chapter, you will learn:
• what a variable is;
• how to write and rewrite algebraic expressions;
• how to compare two complicated algebraic expressions;
• how to solve for a variable if you know that two expressions are equal; and
• how to determine when there is one solution, multiple solutions, and no solution.

Chapter 7 Slope and Association

Previously, you used histograms and box plots to look for Patterns in Data and answer statistical questions. In this chapter, you will continue to look for Patterns in Data while also investigating Inputs and Outputs as you learn how to use scatter plots to determine relationships between two different measures for a set of objects. For example, you could analyze amounts of fertilizer and plant height to answer a question like, "If I give a plant more fertilizer, will it grow taller?"

In Section 7.2, you will use Linear Relationships and Connections Across Representations to examine races called triathlons to discover when rates are the same or different. You will also measure the steepness of lines, a concept called "slope," from situations, tables, and graphs.

Finally, you will revisit Patterns in Data to look at how to make predictions about future events from existing data using trend lines and equations. You will learn about the concept of association to help you describe the relationship between two pieces of data.

In this chapter, you will learn how to:
• create scatter plots that show the relationship between two variables;
• identify associations between sets of data and represent the relationship with a trend line;
• measure the steepness of a line by using slope;
• determine the slope of a line given its equation, its graph, or any two points on the line; and
• write the equation of a trend line to fit linear data.

In this chapter, you will learn how to:
• determine the measurements of missing angles made by a line that intersects parallel lines;
• determine unknown angles inside and outside of triangles;
• determine whether two triangles are similar by looking at their angles;
• determine missing side lengths of right triangles using the Right Triangle Theorem;
• determine the square root of a number and identify irrational numbers; and
• convert terminating and repeating decimals to fractions.
